Drop the READ-SIDE ZERO-FILL from #416's bound, keeping the bound itself.

Two deleted lines inside diskimage__internal_access() (diskimage.c:1481-1482).  A
refused access still returns 0; the caller's buffer is simply left as it was.

Chosen over the more obvious mutants for a measured reason: it leaves the REFUSAL
intact, so all 43 rows that assert "the access was refused" stay GREEN, and only the
two rows that assert what happens to the CALLER'S BYTES go red.  MEASURED, 45 rows /
0 failures pristine, 45 rows / 2 failures mutant, and the two are the same property at
two sites -- the past-EOF read in SECTION 2 and the negative-offset read in H2.

It is the edit a maintainer might make in good faith: "we return failure, so the caller
must not look at the buffer -- the memset is wasted work on an error path."  The code's
own comment at diskimage.c:1469-1473 exists to refute exactly that, because dev_wdc's
read path declares a 32 KB buffer ON THE STACK, discards this function's return value
and copies the whole buffer to the guest.  Same shape as the diskimage_sync mutant,
whose "fsync already flushes, the fflush is redundant" produced a real shipped defect.

Deliberately NOT the bound itself.  Measured on three variants -- writes bounded by
ADVERTISED capacity rather than the backed extent, a START-ONLY bound, and dropping
`offset < 0` -- each turns four to six rows red across three sections, which proves
only that deleting a bound breaks the rows written for it.

THE ALTERNATIVE THAT WAS MEASURED AND NOT CHOSEN, recorded so it is not rediscovered:
`lendone < (ssize_t)len` -> `lendone <= 0` at diskimage.c:1537 -- the pre-#416 shape
that the comment directly above it names.  It kills EXACTLY ONE row ("[PROOF] short
write (256 of 1024 absorbed) returns failure") and is equally plausible in good faith,
so on row count alone it is the better pin.  It was rejected because its kill depends
on RLIMIT_FSIZE producing a genuine PARTIAL write: on a host where the store absorbs 0
bytes instead of 256 the mutant SURVIVES and the control reports SELFMUTANT_FAIL -- a
red row that reads exactly like a dead detector when it is the fixture that failed.
A control whose colour depends on the environment is the gate_ab wall-clock lesson in
miniature; determinism was preferred to a one-row pin.
